            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The cloud primarily symbolizes the uncreated divine presence and glory of God (the Shekinah) that simultaneously reveals and conceals Him.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The cloud symbolizes a heavenly vehicle or transport, carrying 'the word' (as rain) and composed of a multitude of saints 'risen from the sea of peoples'.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Divine presence and glory (Shekinah).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A vehicle of heaven, carriers of the word, and an airborne multitude.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus sharply distinguishes the theophanic 'glory cloud' from the 'cloud of witnesses' (viewed as a separate crowd metaphor). The book fuses these, treating the multitude and the vehicle as the unified substance and function of the symbol.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus identifies the primary referent as God's own manifest presence. The book replaces this with created or functional components: a vehicle/transport, a multitude of people (saints), and a cargo of 'the word' (doctrine/rain).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"While the book references the exact same events (the pillar of cloud, the ascension, Christ's return, Hebrews 12), it reinterprets them. Instead of viewing the cloud as the Shekinah glory, it systematizes the symbol around the concept of transport, linking the meteorological imagery (rain) to doctrine and the physical formation of clouds (rising from the sea) to multitudes of people.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The texts concerning the Tabernacle and Temple (Exodus 40, 1 Kings 8) explicitly identify the cloud with the 'glory of the LORD' filling the space, representing God's personal, uncreated presence rather than a multitude of saints or a vehicle carrying the word.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
